I'm probably like, yeah, it's okay.
我当时大概会说，好吧，这还不错。

00:41:34.492 --> 00:41:37.401
Rule of writing number one, everything's better with a ghost.
写作第一法则，加个鬼魂什么都好说。

00:41:37.401 --> 00:41:38.403
You know what I mean?
你知道我的意思吗？

00:41:38.380 --> 00:41:40.029
It just makes it very exciting.
这会让事情变得非常刺激。

00:41:40.029 --> 00:41:46.838
There's a ghost in this episode and you're like, oh, I didn't know that they're doing ghosts on the CBS sitcom at eight o'clock.
这一集里有个鬼魂，然后你会觉得，哦，我不知道他们要在CBS情景喜剧里在八点钟档搞鬼魂这一套。

00:41:46.838 --> 00:41:48.860
I didn't know we're doing Hamlet here.
我不知道我们在这里演《哈姆雷特》。

00:41:48.860 --> 00:41:52.112
I'd like to point out that I think I could be wrong.
我想指出的是，我可能记错了。

00:41:52.112 --> 00:41:56.991
I think this might be your first right, right after the original right, right?
我觉得这可能是你们第一次“对，对”，就在最初的那个“对，对”之后，对吧？

00:41:56.991 --> 00:41:57.992
And it's subtle.
而且这很微妙。

00:41:57.992 --> 00:42:05.059
It's right as you're getting on top of of Rod, Ted again, Robin, you're kissing, and she says something, can you?
就在你压住罗宾——泰德再次压住罗宾，你们在接吻，然后她说了一些话，你能吗？

00:42:05.059 --> 00:42:07.460
I can't believe that we're making it right, right?
我不敢相信我们真的做到了，对吧？

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:09.780
It's great.
太棒了。

00:42:09.780 --> 00:42:11.900
You guys were really electric in that whole thing.
你们俩在整段戏里的表现都极具张力。

00:42:11.900 --> 00:42:13.619
It was just like, what's gonna happen?
简直就是，接下来会发生什么？

00:42:13.619 --> 00:42:15.059
It's a suspense episode.
这是一集悬疑片。

00:42:15.059 --> 00:42:16.059
It's a ghost story.
这是一个鬼故事。

00:42:16.059 --> 00:42:19.219
It's a little bit of a horror movie, and there's a Korean Elvis.
还有点恐怖片的味道，而且里面还有一个韩国猫王。

00:42:19.219 --> 00:42:22.900
It kind of is everything you could ever ask for in 22 minutes of TV, I think.
我觉得这基本上涵盖了你在22分钟电视剧里能要求的一切。

00:42:22.900 --> 00:42:23.739
I like this one.
我很喜欢这一集。

00:42:23.739 --> 00:42:27.300
I loved when Marshall says, you just got burned Phonics style.
我最喜欢马歇尔说：“你刚刚用自然拼读法被喷了。”

00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:28.460
Phonics style.
自然拼读法。

00:42:28.460 --> 00:42:30.940
I guarantee you that was Chris or Phil.
我向你保证那是克里斯（Chris）或菲尔（Phil）想的。

00:42:30.940 --> 00:42:31.860
That was for you.
那是专门给你的。

00:42:31.860 --> 00:42:32.699
That could be a huge one.
这可能是一个很大的梗。

00:42:32.699 --> 00:42:34.980
It could have been me, but I think it was Lord and Miller.
也可能是我，但我认为是菲尔和克里斯。

00:42:34.980 --> 00:42:39.699
Because if that was me, it's just me stealing their verbatim, I think, their vernacular.
因为如果那是我，那也只是我原封不动地偷用了他们的原话，他们的日常用语。

00:42:39.699 --> 00:42:42.019
That's a very Chris and Phil shout out to them.
这绝对是克里斯和菲尔的风格，向他们致敬。

00:42:42.019 --> 00:42:49.690
When did you guys come up with the kind of ultimate plot point, which is the phone switcheroo?
你们是什么时候想出那种终极情节转折点的，也就是手机调包恶作剧？

00:42:49.690 --> 00:42:56.369
I mean, again, I think technologically, we were like, sometimes that happens because it's 2006.
我的意思是，再说一次，我觉得在技术层面上，我们当时就觉得，有时候事情就是那样，因为那是2006年。

00:42:56.369 --> 00:43:02.610
I think it was how do we, what really undoes Ted here, and it could really only be that, right?
我觉得当时是我们在想，究竟是什么，是什么真正毁了这里的泰德，而且真的只能是那个，对吧？

00:43:02.610 --> 00:43:07.769
I think it was just by process of elimination, like what can, what will destroy everything?
我觉得这只是通过排除法得出的，比如有什么能、什么会摧毁一切？

00:43:07.769 --> 00:43:10.769
If Robin hears directly from Victoria, it destroys everything.
如果罗宾直接从维多利亚那里听到消息，那就一切都毁了。

00:43:10.769 --> 00:43:13.090
And we just, I think concocted a way that could happen.
而我们只是，我觉得我们捏造了一种可能发生的方式。

00:43:13.090 --> 00:43:14.289
She's not gonna walk in the door.
她不会自己走进来。

00:43:14.289 --> 00:43:18.889
She didn't get on a plane from Germany to come surprise Ted at Robin's apartment at him, right?
她没有坐飞机从德国来罗宾的公寓给泰德一个惊喜，对吧？

00:43:18.889 --> 00:43:19.969
It had to be that.
只能是那样。

00:43:19.969 --> 00:43:21.449
I think we just got there.
我想我们就是这么想到的。

00:43:21.449 --> 00:43:29.130
But when we got, when watching it last night for the first time in many years, I was, I was delighted by it because I was like surprising, but inevitable.
但当我们，当昨天时隔多年第一次重看它时，我，我对此感到很高兴，因为我觉得这很意外，但又在情理之中。

00:43:29.489 --> 00:43:29.730
Yep.
没错。

00:43:29.730 --> 00:43:36.210
And maybe a little technologically outdated, but, uh, but it works, right?
也许在技术上有点过时了，但是，呃，不过它奏效了，对吧？

00:43:36.210 --> 00:43:36.570
It works.
它奏效了。

00:43:36.570 --> 00:43:44.409
We never said it was in 2006, but when he, when he comes out there and, and she's on the phone and her whole energy is so different.
我们从没说过那是2006年，但当他，当他走到外面，然后，她正在打电话，她的整个状态完全不同。

00:43:44.769 --> 00:43:53.409
And I, you know, I remember, uh, Ben Lee, my, my dear friend, who I used to make music with as Radner and Lee as I'm, it's plugging my other project.
然后，你知道，我记得，呃，本·李，我的，我的挚友，我以前曾和他一起做音乐，当时叫拉德纳和李，顺便宣传一下我的其他项目。

00:43:53.409 --> 00:44:04.289
But he told me that we used to talk about how bad we are or would be at being like bad dudes.
但他告诉我，我们以前经常讨论我们在扮演混蛋这方面有多糟糕，或者会有多糟糕。

00:44:04.289 --> 00:44:14.690
Like I never wrote this song, but I thought it was kind of like a good line for a country song to say I dance poorly when I dance with the devil.
就像我从来没写过这首歌，但我觉得对于乡村歌曲来说，这算是一句不错的歌词：当我与魔鬼共舞时，我舞姿拙劣。

00:44:14.690 --> 00:44:17.050
Oh, that is a good line.
哦，那真是一句好词。

00:44:17.050 --> 00:44:20.090
Like my moves don't work when I'm dancing with the devil.
就像当我与魔鬼共舞时，我的舞步就不起作用了。

00:44:20.090 --> 00:44:27.489
And Ben told me, he said, you know, I tried on like the costume of like a dickish rocker for like a couple months.
本告诉我，他说，你知道，我曾试着假装穿上混蛋摇滚乐手的戏服，大概持续了几个月。

00:44:27.489 --> 00:44:29.230
And everyone was like, what are you doing?
然后每个人都说，你在干什么？

00:44:29.230 --> 00:44:30.309
You can't do this.
你不能这么做。

00:44:30.309 --> 00:44:31.309
You can't do this.
你不能这么做。

00:44:31.309 --> 00:44:34.849
But other people who that's their whole brand, they just start off, they do it.
但其他人，这本就是他们的全部人设，他们一开始就这么做，他们就是这么干的。

00:44:34.849 --> 00:44:38.650
And not only do they do it, they seem to get away with it for quite a while, right?
不仅如此，他们似乎还能逍遥法外很长一段时间，对吧？

00:44:38.650 --> 00:44:39.650
Yeah.
是啊。

00:44:39.650 --> 00:44:40.650
Yes.
没错。

00:44:40.650 --> 00:44:46.409
But if you're, if you're constitutionally a nice, kind, thoughtful person, it's going to eat you alive.
但如果你，如果你生性是个善良、仁慈、体贴的人，这会把你活活折磨死。

00:44:46.409 --> 00:44:48.250
Karma gets you quicker.
因果报应来得更快。

00:44:48.250 --> 00:45:19.989
Karma gets you much faster and I feel like how many times is Barney like given a false name had a whole false thing and he just seems to get away with it because he never claimed to be anything different whereas Ted actually the ethics does matter to him he does want to be a good guy he is constitutionally honest and when he tries to deceive Victoria and himself on a huge level and Robin, it boomerang so fast back at him.
因果报应会更快地找上你，我觉得巴尼有多少次用假名字、编造一整套谎言，而他似乎总能逃脱惩罚，因为他从没标榜自己是什么好人，而泰德，道德对他来说真的很重要，他确实想成为一个好人，他生性诚实，当他试图在极大程度上欺骗维多利亚、他自己以及罗宾时，报应以极快的速度反弹到了他身上。

00:45:19.989 --> 00:45:31.809
It's really true because like Barney, even though there's definitely a heart and soul in there as we've discussed and as we see as the seasons go on, Barney's in the armor building business.
这真的千真万确，因为就像巴尼一样，尽管正如我们所讨论的以及随着剧集发展我们所看到的，他的内心深处其实是有灵魂和温情的，但巴尼一直在从事建立心灵盔甲的买卖。

00:45:31.809 --> 00:45:56.136
Barney's building armor starting with that suit and the more bad crap you do, the more you build up your armor I think and when you're the guy that doesn't do the bad stuff and do it once, you get burned and it hits you and there's no armor there and that's kind of what the end of this episode is and why it's why a future into narrator Ted frames the story the way he does.
巴尼从那套西装开始就在构筑盔甲，你做的混蛋事越多，我猜你的盔甲就越厚；而当你是个平时不做坏事、偶尔做一次的人时，你就会遭到反噬，它会狠狠击中你，而那里根本没有盔甲，这正是本集结尾的意义所在，也是为什么未来的旁白泰德会以那种方式来构建这个故事。

00:45:56.136 --> 00:45:57.110
He gets burned.
他遭到了反噬。

00:45:57.110 --> 00:46:06.859
But that's what I mean though about how I met your mother being a moral or ethical universe where you can't get away with it that long.
但这就是我的意思，关于《老爸老妈浪漫史》是一个怎样的道德或伦理世界，在那里你不可能逃脱惩罚太久。

00:46:06.859 --> 00:46:10.903
There's some sort of come up and for unethical behavior.
不道德的行为总会有某种报应。

00:46:10.903 --> 00:46:14.586
By the way, here's another good country song title.
顺便说一句，这里有另一个不错的乡村歌曲歌名。

00:46:14.586 --> 00:46:16.969
Nothing good happens after 2 a.m.
凌晨两点之后没有好事发生。

00:46:16.969 --> 00:46:20.570
Please somebody write that country song and then send it to us.
请哪位写一下这首乡村歌曲，然后寄给我们。

00:46:20.570 --> 00:46:21.571
It's a good one.
这是一个好歌名。

00:46:21.571 --> 00:46:33.525
We have reached a point in our show that we like to call questions and observations from a clinical psychologist who's never seen how I met your mother and also happens to be married to Josh.
我们的节目现在进行到一个环节，我们喜欢称之为“来自从未看过《老爸老妈浪漫史》的临床心理学家的提问与观察，她碰巧还是乔许的妻子”。

00:46:33.525 --> 00:46:38.031
I like this episode because it shows Ted wrestling with a moral dilemma.
我喜欢这一集，因为它展现了泰德与道德困境的抗争。

00:46:38.031 --> 00:46:40.284
We get to see is conscience at work.
我们得以看到他的良知在起作用。

00:46:40.284 --> 00:46:49.046
Lillian Marshall sort of represent the angel on his shoulder, his moral compass, and Barney is, you know, a little more devilish, let's say.
莉莉和马歇尔有点像他肩上的天使，代表着他的道德罗盘，而巴尼，你知道，可以说是更像个小恶魔。

00:46:49.046 --> 00:47:05.569
And it's interesting to watch Ted stuck in between with Victoria as a literal manifestation of him negotiating between the two parts of himself, the part that just wants to say whatever and be with Robin and feels like the right thing to do is to follow his heart.
看着泰德夹在中间，而维多利亚作为他内心两部分之间博弈的实体化呈现，这非常有趣：一部分只想不管不顾地和罗宾在一起，觉得遵从内心才是正确的事；

00:47:05.569 --> 00:47:09.950
And the part that knows that the right thing to do is to to be honest.
而另一部分则深知正确的事应该是坦诚相告。

00:47:09.949 --> 00:47:18.309
And I just think as an audience member, watching Ted go through this makes him even more multi-dimensional, even more human.
而我只是觉得，作为观众，看着泰德经历这一切，让他的形象变得更加多维、更加人性化。

00:47:18.309 --> 00:47:21.909
We all have that devil and angel on our shoulders.
我们每个人的肩膀上都有那个恶魔和天使。

00:47:21.909 --> 00:47:23.269
It's relatable.
这很有共鸣。

00:47:23.269 --> 00:47:28.829
And then even further watching him mess up and get burned, that too is relatable.
而且更进一步地看他搞砸事情并遭到反噬，这也是能够引发共鸣的。

00:47:28.829 --> 00:47:36.789
So I just think this episode while painful to watch goes a long way in making Ted a character that feels more real to us.
所以我只是觉得这一集虽然看起来很痛苦，但在让泰德成为一个对我们而言更真实的角色方面，起到了很大的作用。

00:47:36.789 --> 00:47:39.829
That's so smart, and that was very much the intention.
太精辟了，这绝对是我们的初衷。

00:47:39.829 --> 00:47:50.670
And we've said it a couple of times this season, it's really interesting the moments we picked to show that Ted messes up to, and that he's not gonna be giving some amazing speech every week, he's not always going to want the right thing.
我们在这个季度已经说过好几次了，我们精心挑选这些时刻来展现泰德也会搞砸事情，而且他不会每周都发表什么精彩绝伦的演讲，他也不总是会做出正确的事情。

00:47:50.670 --> 00:47:56.630
Underneath all of this nefariousness in this episode is something true-hearted and sweet.
在这集的种种卑劣行径背后，隐藏着一些真挚而甜美的东西。

00:47:56.630 --> 00:48:01.349
At the core of it, Ted really does love Robin, and he really did love her before he ever met Victoria.
归根结底，泰德确实爱罗宾，而且早在遇到维多利亚之前他就已经爱上她了。

00:48:01.349 --> 00:48:04.510
But he's not doing the right thing here, and I do think that humanized the same.
但他在这里做的不是正确的事，我确实认为这让人性化了许多。

00:48:04.510 --> 00:48:12.829
I, this is one of my favorite 10 episodes of season one and maybe of the series because of what exactly what Jordan, I couldn't say it better myself.
我，这是我最喜欢的《老爸老妈浪漫史》第一季的十集之一，也许也是整部剧中我最喜欢的之一，正是因为乔丹所说的那些，我自己都说不出比这更好的了。

00:48:12.829 --> 00:48:15.989
That's what makes this one so interesting and it's Ted so human.
这就是让这一集如此有趣的原因，它让泰德显得如此有人情味。

00:48:15.989 --> 00:48:22.829
And I feel like your performance in this one, you felt it, you felt excited acting in this episode and I could see more.
我觉得你在这集里的表演，你感受到了，你在这一集里演得很带劲，我能看出更多。

00:48:22.829 --> 00:48:25.110
Well, it's also true.
嗯，这也是事实。

00:48:25.110 --> 00:48:38.989
Like if I, if someone presents themselves to me as this kind of like infallible kind of exemplar of moral rectitude, I find it actually not only boring, but like quite suspicious, right?
比如如果我，如果有人向我展示他自己是一个无可挑剔的道德正直典范，我不仅会觉得无聊，而且会觉得相当可疑，对吧？

00:48:38.989 --> 00:48:47.630
I think there's something about, like, piety is very kind of overrated and annoying.
我觉得某种程度上，假正经是非常被高估且令人讨厌的。

00:48:47.630 --> 00:48:53.670
But if someone presents, hey, I'm vulnerable, I'm flawed, let me tell you three ways I messed up.
但如果有人展现出，嘿，我很脆弱，我有缺点，让我告诉你我搞砸过的三件事。

00:48:53.670 --> 00:48:54.670
Do you want to connect?
你想建立连接吗？

00:48:54.670 --> 00:48:55.670
Do you want to have a conversation?
你想聊聊吗？

00:48:55.670 --> 00:48:57.369
Or can I tell you what I learned?
或者我可以告诉你我学到了什么吗？

00:48:57.369 --> 00:49:16.769
That is how we solicit kind of, we get people on our team by showing how human we are, not how perfect we are, infallible we are, that all that does is kind of make us turn on ourselves and go, oh, I'm not like this guy, I'm not like this person, right?
这才是我们如何寻求认同的方式，我们通过展示我们有多么人性化，而不是我们有多完美、多无可挑剔来把人们拉进我们的阵营，因为所有那些完美只会让我们开始自我怀疑并觉得，哦，我可不像这家伙，我可不像这个人，对吧？

00:49:16.769 --> 00:49:24.170
But I think also when you're creating a character that you're presumably, we didn't know at at the time, but gonna follow for eight more seasons.
但我觉得同时，当你塑造一个你大概率要陪伴观众走过接下来八个季度的角色时——虽然当时我们还不知道会这样。

00:49:24.170 --> 00:49:43.730
You wanna feel like they're, I think that's one of the things I'm most proud of about Ted is that he, while his heart was in the right place, his feet were often pointed in the wrong direction, or his head was going one way, his heart was going another way, his dick was going another way.
你希望觉得他们是——我觉得这是我对泰德最引以为傲的一点，那就是尽管他的内心是善良的，但他的双脚往往指向错误的方向，或者他的脑袋想着往一边去，他的心却想着另一边，他的下半身又想着另一个方向。

00:49:43.730 --> 00:49:56.980
He was a man at odds with himself a lot, But he ultimately learned, he had to learn patience, he had to learn, he had to fail more, he had to struggle.
他是一个经常与自己较劲的人，但他最终学会了，他不得不学会耐心，他不得不学会，他必须经历更多的失败，他必须苦苦挣扎。

00:49:57.539 --> 00:50:05.619
And I would say by that when I started playing Ted, I also had to get a little scuffed up more.
我想说的是，当我开始扮演泰德时，我也必须让自己经历更多磕磕绊绊。

00:50:07.019 --> 00:50:18.960
And I think we all, I think that's a good thing to teach, whether it's your kids or anyone else you're teaching like, don't bemoan your failures.
我觉得我们所有人，我觉得这是一件很好的教育事情，无论是对你的孩子还是你教导的任何人，比如不要哀叹你的失败。

00:50:18.960 --> 00:50:21.519
Your failures are where your character gets forged.
你的失败正是塑造你性格的地方。

00:50:21.519 --> 00:50:22.360
Yes.
是的。

00:50:22.360 --> 00:50:29.079
You know, and I think that's what he's telling his kids on some level, like your old man really stepped in it in this one.
你知道，我觉得这也就是他在某种程度上对他的孩子们所说的话，比如你们的老爸这次真的闯大祸了。

00:50:29.079 --> 00:50:30.880
Yeah, and he's not hiding it from them.
是的，而且他并没有对他们隐瞒。

00:50:30.880 --> 00:50:31.960
He's not being pious.
他没有装腔作势。

00:50:31.960 --> 00:50:33.760
He's not being falsely pious.
他没有虚伪地装正经。

00:50:33.760 --> 00:50:34.880
And he's not hiding it from them.
而且他也没有对他们隐瞒这一点。

00:50:34.880 --> 00:50:39.119
And that should solicit a kind of trust from his kids.
这应该会赢得他孩子们的一种信任。

00:50:39.119 --> 00:50:48.519
Like I'm an honest, you know, both my sisters who I think are wonderful mothers, they apologize to their kids.
就像我很诚实，你知道，我的两个姐姐，我都觉得她们是出色的母亲，她们会向自己的孩子道歉。

00:50:48.519 --> 00:50:53.119
They say, I screwed up this time or I'm not gonna get everything right.
她们会说，我这次搞砸了，或者我不可能每件事都做对。

00:50:53.119 --> 00:50:53.960
I'm waiting in it.
我在摸索中前进。

00:50:53.960 --> 00:50:56.000
I'm trying to figure this out as we go.
我正在努力边走边弄明白这一切。

00:50:56.000 --> 00:50:58.519
So I'm really sorry, I think I misjudged that one.
所以我真的很抱歉，我想我那次判断失误了。

00:50:58.519 --> 00:51:01.400
And I think that's the best thing you can do as a parent.
我觉得这是你能作为一个父母做的最好的事。

00:51:01.400 --> 00:51:02.239
That's the best thing you can do.
那是你能做出的最好举动。

00:51:02.239 --> 00:51:05.519
That is very much what I tell my nanny to tell my kids.
这正是我告诉我的保姆转告我孩子们的话。

00:51:05.519 --> 00:51:06.880
I don't really spend much time with my kids.
我其实并没有花太多时间陪我的孩子。

00:51:06.880 --> 00:51:07.719
That's a joke.
开玩笑的。

00:51:07.719 --> 00:51:08.559
That's a joke.
开玩笑的啦。

00:51:08.559 --> 00:51:10.559
You're dad really screwed up this time.
你们的老爸这次真的搞砸了。

00:51:10.559 --> 00:51:16.400
When he's back from his yacht trip, he's going to take you for ice cream.
等他游艇旅行回来后，他会带你们去吃冰淇淋。

00:51:16.400 --> 00:51:41.550
No, that's the best thing you can show your kids, that level of honesty, and I'm a human being and I think future Ted's a good dad for doing that, and he may overshare at times, but I do think that showing your kid that you're a human being is huge because then they get to be too, and that's a gift.
不，这是你能向孩子们展示的最好的事情，那种诚实的程度，我是一个活生生的人，我觉得未来的泰德因为这么做而成为一个好爸爸，他有时可能会分享过多，但我确实认为向你的孩子展示你是一个有人情味的人至关重要，因为这样他们也能成为有人情味的人，而这是一种馈赠。

00:51:41.550 --> 00:51:47.590
Now we've reached the final act of our episode and something we like to do.
现在我们迎来了本集的最后一个环节，也是我们喜欢做的事情。

00:51:47.590 --> 00:51:53.550
On this last part of the episode is read one of the many astonishingly beautiful letters that we receive.
在本集的最后一部分，我们要朗读一封我们收到的令人惊叹的美丽信件。

00:51:53.550 --> 00:51:54.550
We love these.
我们太喜欢这些信了。

00:51:54.550 --> 00:51:55.550
How we made your mother.
老爸老妈浪漫史。

00:51:55.550 --> 00:51:56.550
Yeah, they're so good.
是的，它们太棒了。

00:51:56.550 --> 00:52:11.429
So if you would like, again, a reminder, if you'd like to send us a letter, let us know what the show is meant to you, what your relationship is with the show, or you can write us a letter, You can record an audio, but go to how we made your mother.com.
所以如果你愿意的话，再次提醒一下，如果你想给我们写信，让我们知道这部剧对你意味着什么，你与这部剧的关系是什么，或者你可以给我们写信，你可以录制音频，但请访问 howwemedyourmother.com。

00:52:11.429 --> 00:52:14.230
Go to contact and follow the instructions there.
点击联系页面并按照那里的说明操作。

00:52:14.230 --> 00:52:15.829
Should be pretty clear.
这应该挺清楚的。

00:52:15.829 --> 00:52:18.789
Today's letter is from Shiwon.
今天的信来自始温。

00:52:18.789 --> 00:52:20.710
Hi, my name's Shiwon.
嗨，我叫始温。

00:52:20.710 --> 00:52:25.349
I'm 23 years old and I'm from North Wales, although I'm currently living in London.
我23岁，来自北威尔士，不过目前住在伦敦。

00:52:25.349 --> 00:52:30.630
I've been thinking about what to say here since the podcast came out, but now I have something I really wanna say.
自从这个播客开播以来，我一直在考虑该在这里说些什么，但现在我真的有话想说。

00:52:30.630 --> 00:52:32.349
Trust the process here.
相信这个过程吧。

00:52:32.349 --> 00:52:34.590
I don't remember when I first started watching him.
我不记得自己是什么时候开始看他的。

00:52:34.590 --> 00:52:41.230
They used to air on E4, a British TV channel, when I just started exploring grownup TV for myself.
当时英国电视频道E4经常播出，那时我刚开始探索适合成年人看的电视节目。

00:52:41.230 --> 00:52:42.989
So I must have been around 9 or 10 years old.
所以我当时大概9、10岁左右。

00:52:42.989 --> 00:52:45.309
It's a little young to watch a show, but that's fine.
看这个节目有点太小了，不过没关系。

00:52:45.309 --> 00:52:48.769
But it's safe to say, I don't remember not watching him.
但可以肯定的是，我不记得自己有过不看他的日子。

00:52:48.769 --> 00:52:54.030
For years, I wanted to be a news reporter like Robin, and then I wanted to be a judge like Marshall.
多年来，我想成为像罗宾那样的新闻记者，后来又想成为像马歇尔那样的法官。

00:52:54.030 --> 00:52:58.750
Around the time the podcast first came out, I was applying for a master's course in screenwriting.
就在这个播客刚开播的时候，我正在申请编剧专业的硕士课程。

00:52:58.750 --> 00:53:01.190
I'd been offered an interview and was preparing for it.
我当时已经获得了面试通知，并正在为此做准备。

00:53:01.190 --> 00:53:03.030
I was listening to the podcast.
我在听这个播客。

00:53:03.030 --> 00:53:09.469
I heard Josh talk about the Kierkegaard quote about only being able to live your life forwards and a penny dropped.
我听到乔什谈到克尔凯郭尔的那句名言，大意是生活只能向前看，然后我突然恍然大悟。

00:53:09.469 --> 00:53:12.670
I can't tell you how much that resonated with me.
我无法形容这句话对我的触动有多深。

00:53:12.670 --> 00:53:23.469
Growing up watching him, yum, call it nature or nurture, I found myself to be a person who looks obsessively for signs from the universe and tries her best to romanticize reality.
从小看着他长大，嗯，不管是天性还是后天培养，我发现自己是一个执着于寻找宇宙讯号并竭尽全力将现实浪漫化的人。

00:53:23.469 --> 00:53:25.789
I actually used the Kierkegaard, Josh, quote.
乔什，我真的引用了克尔凯郭尔的话。

00:53:25.789 --> 00:53:29.710
I'm glad I'm getting credited for this quote in my interview.
我很庆幸能在面试中因引用这句话而受到肯定。

00:53:29.710 --> 00:53:30.550
Thank you, Josh.
谢谢你，乔什。

00:53:30.550 --> 00:53:32.150
And I found out today that I got in.
而我今天得知我被录取了。

00:53:32.150 --> 00:53:34.309
All right, congrats.
好吧，恭喜。

00:53:34.309 --> 00:53:46.110
Masters courses and Danish philosophers aside, Hemium has played a really big role in my life from when I'd watch episodes on E4 to when I began to collect the DVDs at age 12 to now my 20s.
抛开硕士课程和丹麦哲学家不谈，从我在E4上看剧，到12岁开始收集DVD，再到如今二十多岁的我，这档节目在我的人生中扮演了非常重要的角色。

00:53:46.110 --> 00:54:00.989
I wasn't the happiest of teenagers, but here was this show that taught me to trust the process of my life, to look for signs from the universe and to romanticize everything in my life from the good to the really crappy, because those were the things that shaped my story.
我并不是一个最快乐的青少年，但就是有这样一部剧，它教导我要相信人生的进程，去寻找来自宇宙的讯号，并将我生活中的一切——从美好的到极其糟糕的——都浪漫化，因为正是这些东西塑造了我的故事。

00:54:00.989 --> 00:54:04.710
At my lowest, I'll still go back to find a speech of Ted's to ground me.
在我处于人生低谷时，我依然会回去找一段泰德的演讲来让自己重回脚踏实地的状态。

00:54:04.710 --> 00:54:10.309
And whenever I see a yellow umbrella in the wild, I take it as a sign to keep trusting the process.
每当我在生活中偶然看到一把黄伞，我都把它当作一个信号，提醒自己继续相信这个过程。

00:54:10.309 --> 00:54:11.840
So thank you.
所以，谢谢你们。

00:54:11.840 --> 00:54:17.239
For a wonderful show that has taught me and so many others that simply, everything will be okay.
感谢这部精彩的节目，它教会了我以及许多其他人一个简单的道理：一切都会好起来的。

00:54:17.239 --> 00:54:27.679
Thanks for this podcast and its help with master's interviews, love from the other side of the Atlantic Shiwon, aspiring screenwriter, no longer aspiring judge.
感谢这个播客以及它对硕士面试的帮助，来自大西洋另一端的爱，始温，立志成为编剧，不再立志成为法官。

00:54:27.679 --> 00:54:28.525
Fantastic.
太棒了。

00:54:28.525 --> 00:54:29.877
Congratulations.
恭喜。

00:54:29.877 --> 00:54:31.229
That's the best.
这真是太棒了。

00:54:31.229 --> 00:54:33.680
I love everything about that.
我喜欢这一切。

00:54:33.679 --> 00:54:35.196
Romanticized reality.
浪漫化的现实。

00:54:35.196 --> 00:54:36.568
I like that phrase.
我喜欢这个短语。

00:54:36.568 --> 00:54:52.960
And, you know, it's a lot of what it's a lot of what we were talking about, you know, like, I think to have a show where everything works out and people just keep going from success to success to success feels really dishonest.
而且，你知道的，这很大程度上就是我们一直在谈论的，你知道的，比如，我认为如果有一部剧，里面的事情总是圆满解决，人们总是一个接一个地走向成功，这会让人觉得很不真实。

00:54:52.960 --> 00:54:57.079
And, and we were trying to, we were trying to make an honest show.
而且，我们一直试图制作一部真实的剧集。

00:54:57.079 --> 00:55:07.119
I am guilty, please equip me, all sins are forgiven, a new York City.
我有罪，请给我定罪，所有罪孽都已被原谅，这里是纽约市。

